BUG REPORT (verbatim from user):
"I'm formatting dates with negative (BCE) years, and the ISO date output comes out mangled:

```js
DateTime.fromObject({ year: -1, month: 1, day: 1, zone: "utc" }).toISODate();
// expected "-0001-01-01", got "00-1-01-01"

DateTime.fromObject({ year: -10, month: 1, day: 1, zone: "utc" }).toISODate();
// expected "-0010-01-01", got "00-10-01-01"
```

The minus sign seems to be counted as one of the digits that get zero-padded, so the year is shifted and the leading zeros end up in the wrong place. Positive years pad correctly, so this only shows up for years before 1 CE."

Your job: find the root cause in /luxon/src (look at the year formatting/padding, likely src/impl/formatter.js `padStart`/`num`/`formatDateTimeFromString` handling of 'yyyy'/'y'/'YYYY' and toISODate in src/datetime.js). The expected behavior: ISO 8601 extended year format for negative years, i.e. a '-' sign followed by at least 4 zero-padded digits ('-0001-01-01', '-0010-01-01'). Fix it minimally so negative years format correctly while positive years and all other formatting stay unchanged. Consider also toISOWeekDate/toISODate/toISO/toFormat('yyyy') consistency, but only fix what is clearly the same bug (the padding of the year sign).

